# Kaggriculture Submission Cadence

- Treat Kaggle ratings as delayed, noisy live-meta evidence. Never judge a new bot from its initial seeding score.
- Submit candidates in batches of exactly two distinct policies: one strongest exploit candidate and one genuinely informative exploration candidate.
- Only the latest two valid submissions remain the active/final pair. A third immediate upload evicts the older bot before its rating matures, so never start the next batch early.
- After a two-bot batch, do not submit a third or fourth bot immediately. Wait at least several hours and inspect completed public games, opponents, gate activation, and rating stability first.
- Start another two-bot batch only after the previous pair has accumulated meaningful evidence or after the quota window has rolled over. Prefer roughly 24 hours for a confident comparison.
- Keep one quota slot available for validation/repair. This is an emergency bound, not a reason to withhold the planned second bot in a batch.
- Two submissions per quota window are autonomous. Use two additional slots only after the preceding pair is stable or 24 hours old; never burn 3-4 slots in one burst.
- Before every upload, run `kaggle competitions submission-limits -c kaggriculture` and inspect current submissions. Record artifact path, byte size, SHA-256, description, timestamp, and returned reference.
- Do not submit an exact duplicate merely because its earlier rating was high unless duplicate-rating calibration is the explicit experiment. Prefer an improved compatible policy or a strategically diverse route.
- After uploading a pair, download and classify their replays before choosing the next candidates.
